        "question_text": "<p>Prove that $\\sin^{2}\\theta + \\cos^{2}\\theta = 1$.</p>",
        "question_image": null,
        "answer_image": null,
        "explanation": "<p>Consider a right-angled triangle with hypotenuse $1$. By definition, $\\sin\\theta = \\frac{\\text{opposite}}{1}$ and $\\cos\\theta = \\frac{\\text{adjacent}}{1}$. Using the Pythagorean theorem: $(\\text{opposite})^2 + (\\text{adjacent})^2 = 1^2$. Thus, $\\sin^{2}\\theta + \\cos^{2}\\theta = 1$.</p>",
        "reference_passage": null,
